  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protecting The Right To Have Access To Adequate Housing</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">As people move to the cities in search of a better life, the worldwide number of slum dwellers has risen to 828 million. In South Africa this trend is accelerating, driving up the price of land and forcing people into substandard high cost housing. The government has committed to polices that would deliver adequate housing and support urban economic development. But many policies are not implemented and of those that are, a large majority are enforced without consultation with the communities involved. As a result poor, vulnerable and marginalised communities continue to face a substantial challenge in realising their right to housing. Those living in informal housing face threats of eviction and suffer from a lack of basic services such as water and sanitation. This project will support South African urban slum communities to secure adequate housing and basic services. Through legal training, advice and action individuals and communities will be able to challenge exploitative landlords and unfulfilled government responsibilities. This project will help them access affordable housing with secure tenure, and adequate basic services. It will also give them the confidence and skills they need to lobby for secure housing.</narrative>
  </description>
